<BaseLayout title="DeKalb Surveillance | NoRec.US" description="Publicly documented surveillance technology and records for DeKalb, Illinois." active="surveillance">
<main class="page_shell">
  <nav class="jurisdiction_breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <a href="/illinois/">Illinois</a><span>→</span><a href="/illinois/counties/dekalb/">Dekalb County</a><span>→</span><strong>DeKalb</strong>
  </nav>
  <header class="article_header">
    <p class="eyebrow">LOCAL SURVEILLANCE · DEKALB, ILLINOIS</p>
    <h1>DeKalb</h1>
    <p class="article_deck">Municipal surveillance research for DeKalb. Unknown or undocumented categories should not be interpreted as absence of surveillance technology.</p>
  </header>
<section>
    <p class="eyebrow">DEKALB POLICE DEPARTMENT · ALPR</p>
    <h2>Genetec first, Flock now documented</h2>
    <div class="finding_grid">
      <article>
        <span class="status_label">DOCUMENTED</span>
        <h3>12 Genetec readers purchased in 2021</h3>
        <p>Resolution 2021-093 authorized the purchase of twelve CDS Genetec license-plate readers for $145,865.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="status_label">DOCUMENTED</span>
        <h3>Program launched in 2022</h3>
        <p>DeKalb's annual reports say the ALPR program launched in April 2022 and regularly recorded more than 50,000 plates per day.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="status_label">DOCUMENTED</span>
        <h3>2026 Flock contract</h3>
        <p>The City Council approved a two-year Flock Safety ALPR service contract for 2026–2027 in an amount not to exceed $27,500.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="status_label">DOCUMENTED POLICY</span>
        <h3>ALPR policy 406.12</h3>
        <p>DeKalb publishes an ALPR policy covering authorized users, law-enforcement uses, alerts, and stored data. The policy states the ALPR system does not use facial recognition.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

<section>
    <p class="eyebrow">DEKALB POLICE DEPARTMENT · AXON</p>
    <h2>A broad 2026–2031 Axon deployment</h2>
    <div class="finding_grid">
      <article>
        <span class="status_label">DOCUMENTED</span>
        <h3>$1.247 million / five years</h3>
        <p>A 2025 Axon agreement lists a 60-month program beginning in January 2026 with a total cost of $1,247,755.20.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="status_label">DOCUMENTED</span>
        <h3>Axon Body 4</h3>
        <p>The agreement's delivery schedule includes dozens of Axon Body 4 cameras, docks, connectivity, Evidence.com storage, auto-tagging, and community-request licenses.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="status_label">DOCUMENTED</span>
        <h3>Fleet 3 in-car video</h3>
        <p>The agreement includes 19 Fleet 3 vehicle systems and interior-camera components. Vendor material included in the city packet describes Fleet 3 as combining evidence capture, ALPR, location, alerts, and livestreaming.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="status_label">DOCUMENTED</span>
        <h3>Five Fleet ALPR licenses</h3>
        <p>The quote includes five “AXON FLEET 3 - ALPR LICENSE - 1 CAMERA” licenses and 19 real-time-location, alert, and livestream licenses.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="status_label">DOCUMENTED</span>
        <h3>Axon Fusus licensing</h3>
        <p>The agreement contains Axon Fusus “Plus User” licenses across the officer-safety-plan bundles. The document establishes licensing; it does not by itself establish every Fusus integration or connected private camera source.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="status_label">DOCUMENTED HISTORY</span>
        <h3>Axon selected after a 2020 pilot</h3>
        <p>DeKalb's 2020 procurement memorandum says officers tested multiple body-camera vendors and unanimously recommended Axon, citing camera operation and Evidence.com.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <details class="surveillance_sources" open>
      <summary>Axon sources</summary>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://www.cityofdekalb.com/DocumentCenter/View/19825/Res-2025-085-Passed-092225-Axon">City of DeKalb — 2025 Axon agreement / quote</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.cityofdekalb.com/DocumentCenter/View/11800/13-Res-20-106">DeKalb Police — 2020 Axon body-camera procurement memorandum</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.cityofdekalb.com/DocumentCenter/View/11414/40711---Body-Worn-Camera-Policy">DeKalb Police — Body-Worn Camera Policy 407.11</a></li>
      </ul>
    </details>
  </section>

<section>
    <p class="eyebrow">DRONES</p>
    <h2>County and municipal UAS programs</h2>
    <div class="finding_grid">
      <article>
        <span class="status_label">DOCUMENTED</span>
        <h3>DeKalb County Sheriff: three drones</h3>
        <p>The SFY 2024 statewide drone report lists three Sheriff's Office drones and seven flights, including missing-person searches, fire assessment, crash documentation, and a suicidal-subject response.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="status_label">DOCUMENTED</span>
        <h3>DeKalb Police: one drone</h3>
        <p>The same statewide report lists DeKalb Police as owning one drone. The city's current UAS policy was revised in March 2026.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="status_label">DOCUMENTED POLICY</span>
        <h3>Sheriff UAS safeguards</h3>
        <p>The Sheriff's published UAS policy addresses statutory restrictions, facial-recognition use, prohibited random surveillance, weaponization, retention, annual reporting, and disclosure.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="status_label">DOCUMENTED POLICY</span>
        <h3>City UAS safeguards</h3>
        <p>DeKalb Police policy 407.12 references the Freedom from Drone Surveillance Act, the Drones as First Responders Act, privacy rights, FAA requirements, and statutory exclusions for protected demonstrations from defined event categories.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

<section>
    <p class="eyebrow">COMMUNITY CAMERA REGISTRY</p>
    <h2>Private-camera locations can be registered with police</h2>
    <p>DeKalb Police operates a voluntary camera-registration program. The department states registration does not provide direct access to a participant's cameras; officers may identify nearby registered systems and request relevant footage.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="evidence_box">
    <span class="status_label">CONTRIBUTE</span>
    <p>This jurisdiction file is community-maintained. Primary records, corrections, and missing technology categories are welcome.</p>
    <a class="button button_light" href="https://github.com/yung-megafone/NoRec-US">Contribute on GitHub</a>
  </section>
</main>
</BaseLayout>
